            <p>None of it happens in isolation, either. Back in Rome I've still got a small group of friends I've known since I was fourteen &mdash; the kind of people who've turned into family, through the rough patches as much as the good ones. Moving to the Netherlands didn't shrink that circle, it just made it bigger and a lot more international. I still fly back regularly to see everyone in Rome, and over here I've ended up meeting some of the most genuinely intelligent people I know, a few of whom I now consider close friends too and still make a point of visiting.</p>

            <p>Somewhere in the middle of all that I ended up with a Lada Niva &mdash; a boxy little 4x4 with basically none of the electronics a modern car has, which is exactly why I love it. I found it a few years ago, and it's been a slow, ongoing project ever since: a good chunk of my free time goes into keeping it running, fixing whatever breaks on it (usually on the ground, usually with whatever tools happen to be in reach), and taking it off-road every chance I get. I've written more about why I think that simplicity is a feature and not a limitation <a href="/articles/posts/old-things-built-to-last/">here</a>, but the short version is: I can actually understand this car, top to bottom, and there's very little else in my life I can say that about.</p>

